Chapter: ProbeClosureMutual

 Is the predicate really a SELF tail call, or anything that looks like a
 loop? Two cells, cheapest control first, because a fault halts.

 `pick` is a control: a function-returning definition with two return
 paths and no recursion at all. If it faults, the conditional is doing
 the work and the whole recursion story is wrong.

 `even-fn` and `odd-fn` are MUTUAL tail recursion. Neither contains a
 call to itself, so a self-call test sees nothing in either, and neither
 should be compiled as a loop.

 `picked 47` then `mutual 47` means the predicate is exactly the self
 tail call. `picked 47` then a fault means mutual tail recursion breaks
 it too and the predicate is wider than any self-call test.

Section: Shapes

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

 Two return paths, no recursion.

  pick : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  pick (n) = if n == 0 then add3 5 else add3 6

 Mutual tail recursion, neither definition calling itself.

  even-fn : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  even-fn (n) = if n == 0 then add3 5 else odd-fn (n - 1)

  odd-fn : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  odd-fn (n) = even-fn n

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("picked " & show (((pick 0) 20) 22))
    print-line-uni ("mutual " & show (((even-fn 4) 20) 22))
  end
